          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "Yes, they differ, mainly in how the blood-pressure range is labeled and in how quickly people are offered medication.\n\n**The labels differ.** The retrieved guidance says the 130/80 mm Hg diagnostic threshold is a US position. European guidance starts office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats readings of 130–139 / 80–89 as \"high-normal\" rather than stage 1 disease. The same reading can therefore be labeled differently depending on which body's guidance is used. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.)\n\n**The approach to low-risk stage 1 differs.** Because of that threshold difference, the retrieved guidance says much of the population that US guidance calls stage 1 is instead advised on lifestyle changes alone under European guidance. The sources describe the two bodies as differing on how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension. (Source: *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*.)\n\n**What the results did not cover:** They do not give the specific criteria either body uses to decide when medication starts for a given person, such as risk scores or how long lifestyle measures are tried. They also do not say which approach is better for any individual. Those questions are worth raising with a doctor, who can weigh a person's own risk factors and readings.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
